WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.599
Let's get into a question that every serious user hits, eventually.

2
00:00:04.599 --> 00:00:07.920
Some files should never reach clogged code.

3
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.760
Your .env, your private keys, a folder of customer secrets.

4
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.960
Get this wrong and a credential can slip into context.

5
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.000
This lesson walks through the real exclusion mechanism, how setting precedent decides what

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.120
wins, and the myths worth retiring.

7
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.600
Let's get into it.

8
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:33.319
A single deny rule decides whether your .env stays private or lands in the model's context.

9
00:00:33.319 --> 00:00:38.720
A deny rule is a read entry in the permissions block of settings.json.

10
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:45.520
It tells clogged code not to read a matching path, and it is the real mechanism for excluding files.

11
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.000
The settings hierarchy is how your settings files layer together.

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.459
Managed local, project, and user settings each contribute rules, and higher precedence files win.

13
00:00:56.459 --> 00:01:00.599
The glob pattern is a path expression with wildcards.

14
00:01:00.599 --> 00:01:03.360
One star matches a single segment of a path.

15
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.040
Two stars match across directories, so you can name one file or an entire tree.

16
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.199
Every rule you write combines all three, and the way they interact decides exactly which

17
00:01:14.199 --> 00:01:18.180
files clogged code can and can't open.

18
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.360
So here is the actual mechanism.

19
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:26.319
File exclusion lives in the permissions block of settings.json as read deny entries.

20
00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:32.459
You write an entry like read of .env, and clogged code will refuse to read that path.

21
00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:36.580
The patterns use glob syntax borrowed from .getignore.

22
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:39.019
One star matches a single path segment.

23
00:01:39.019 --> 00:01:44.180
Two stars cross directory boundaries, so you can match a whole nested tree.

24
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:45.300
Anchors set the base.

25
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.940
A bare path resolves relative to the working directory.

26
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.620
A leading slash anchors to the directory of the settings file holding the rule, so it

27
00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:59.480
is the project root for a project file and your home clogged directory for a user file.

28
00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.919
A double slash gives you a truly absolute path.

29
00:02:02.919 --> 00:02:04.839
Only the syntax is borrowed, though.

30
00:02:04.839 --> 00:02:10.679
There is no separate ignore file, just entries inside settings.json.

31
00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:15.860
Credential protection is the highest value reason to write deny rules, so start there.

32
00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:23.039
For environment files, deny read of .env, and deny read of .env.* to catch variants

33
00:02:23.039 --> 00:02:25.580
like .env.local.

34
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:31.699
For secret directories, deny read of a secrets folder, followed by a two star wildcard, which

35
00:02:31.699 --> 00:02:34.600
blocks everything nested inside it.

36
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:40.500
For home credentials, deny read of your ssh folder and your cloud credential folders,

37
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.259
using a tilde to anchor at home.

38
00:02:43.259 --> 00:02:48.619
These files hold API keys, database passwords, and private keys.

39
00:02:48.619 --> 00:02:54.919
If they never enter context, they can never leak into generated code or a suggestion.

40
00:02:54.919 --> 00:02:59.960
Writing a handful of deny rules for these paths is the single highest impact configuration

41
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.360
step for keeping secrets out of clod code.

42
00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.600
Now where do these rules live?

43
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.360
Settings come from four layers, and it helps to know them highest first.

44
00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.240
Managed settings are organization-enforced policy.

45
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.679
They sit at the top and can't be overridden locally.

46
00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:24.860
Local settings are your git-ignored local file for personal overrides that stay off the repository.

47
00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:31.059
Project settings are the committed file that ships with the repo, so they are shared team rules.

48
00:03:31.059 --> 00:03:37.619
And user settings live in your home directory as personal defaults that apply across every project.

49
00:03:37.619 --> 00:03:40.220
The same deny rule can appear in any of these.

50
00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.380
For ordinary settings, a higher layer overrides a lower one.

51
00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.639
But permission rules work differently.

52
00:03:46.639 --> 00:03:52.300
They merge across all four layers, and we'll see next why a deny in any layer wins over

53
00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.679
an allow in any other.

54
00:03:54.679 --> 00:03:58.919
Precedence works differently for permission rules than for ordinary settings, and it is

55
00:03:58.919 --> 00:04:00.740
worth being precise.

56
00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:05.759
For ordinary non-permission settings, a higher layer simply overrides a lower one.

57
00:04:05.759 --> 00:04:09.679
So managed beats local beats project beats user.

58
00:04:09.679 --> 00:04:14.800
But permission rules, the allow, ask, and deny entries, don't work that way.

59
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.359
They merge across all four layers.

60
00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:22.200
Every deny from every layer is collected, and deny is evaluated before allow.

61
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:28.820
So a deny in any layer, even your user file, blocks an allow in a higher layer, like managed.

62
00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.600
If a path matches any deny rule anywhere, it is blocked.

63
00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.239
And no allow rule can reopen it.

64
00:04:35.239 --> 00:04:36.720
That is the key idea.

65
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.640
Deny beats allow across layers.

66
00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.720
That tells you exactly why a given file is blocked or open.

67
00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:47.380
Deny rules are powerful, but you should know their boundary.

68
00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.399
Deny blocks Claude Code's built-in tools.

69
00:04:50.399 --> 00:04:56.899
That means read, edit, and shell, reads like cat or head are all stopped for a denied path.

70
00:04:56.899 --> 00:04:59.160
It also blocks indirect access.

71
00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:03.679
So an at mention of the file and an editor selection are covered too.

72
00:05:03.679 --> 00:05:07.279
What deny doesn't block is an arbitrary subprocess.

73
00:05:07.279 --> 00:05:12.880
If Claude Code runs a Python script, that script can still open a denied file, because

74
00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.200
deny governs the tools Claude Code drives, not the operating system underneath.

75
00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.880
That is a real gap to understand.

76
00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:27.239
When you need the file to be unreadable, no matter what runs, deny rules alone aren't enough.

77
00:05:27.239 --> 00:05:32.399
You reach for sandboxing, which enforces access at the file system level, and stops the shell

78
00:05:32.399 --> 00:05:35.119
itself from succeeding.

79
00:05:35.119 --> 00:05:40.000
Let's retire three myths, because they all come up constantly, and they are all wrong.

80
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.739
The first is the .claudeignore file.

81
00:05:42.739 --> 00:05:44.119
There is no such file.

82
00:05:44.119 --> 00:05:46.339
It has never existed as a feature.

83
00:05:46.339 --> 00:05:50.739
If you see advice telling you to create one, that advice is fabricated.

84
00:05:50.739 --> 00:05:52.940
The real answer is a deny rule.

85
00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:58.260
The second myth is that a .gitignore controls what Claude Code can read. It doesn't.

86
00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:03.700
A file being ignored by git says nothing about whether Claude Code can read it.

87
00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.059
Ignored files are still fully readable unless you deny them.

88
00:06:07.059 --> 00:06:11.220
There is also no magicignore environment variable that hides files.

89
00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:16.019
If you want a path excluded, there is exactly one supported path to it.

90
00:06:16.019 --> 00:06:19.940
You write a read-deny rule in settings.json.

91
00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:23.739
Everything else is a misconception.

92
00:06:23.739 --> 00:06:29.500
Exclusion is really a permission decision, not a hidden list sitting somewhere in your repo.

93
00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:36.660
What makes the whole model work is that deny always wins, no matter which settings layer it comes from.

94
00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:41.140
Hold on to that, and the myths stop being tempting and confusing, and your credentials

95
00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:43.540
stay where you need them to be.

96
00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:44.660
That's it for this lesson.

97
00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:45.940
I'll see you in the next video.

